fihrist:manuscript_7092 a mdhn:Manuscript ;
    rdfs:label "Persian MS 593" ;
    dct:date "1674-08-16" ;
    dct:description """Zād al-Musāfirīn (Provisions for the Travellers) by medieval Sufi poet Amīr Ḥusayn ibn ‘Ālim ibn Ḥasan al-Ḥusaynī Haravī, known as Amīr Ḥusaynī or Fakhr al-Sādāt (d. ca. 1317).
                     Originally from Ghor, Afghanistan, the author travelled to Multan where he became a disciple of the Suhravardī Ṣūfī Shaykh, Rukn al-Dīn Abū'l-Fatḥ, the grandson and successor of Bahā' al-Dīn Zakarīya, but then he ultimately settled in Herat. 
                     Comprised of eight maqālāt (treatises), the author modelled his work on the Ḥadīqat al-Ḥaqīqat (Enclosed Garden of Truth) of Ḥakīm Sanā'ī . 
                     A scribe named Shīr Muḥammad Dihlavī likely completed this volume in the Indian subcontinent in 1085 AH (1674 CE).""" ;
    dct:identifier "manuscript_7092" ;
    dct:language "fa" ;
    dct:spatial "Indian subcontinent" ;
    dct:type "codex" ;
    mdhn:digitallyPublishedBy glam:publisher_The_University_of_Manchester_Library ;
    mdhn:hasContribution contrib:manuscript_7092_person_121710001_aut_Persian_MS_593_item1,
